# Welcome to Pixl!

Pixl is where you build something real, ship it, and get rewarded for it.

You take a sidequest (or build your own thing), ship real projects over the next few months, and earn pixels, real prizes, and help rebuild the world. This page points you to the right place to start.

Everything runs inside a story about a broken digital world called **Origin**, shattered by something called the **Great Static** and slowly being rebuilt as Pixl.

You don't need to care about all the lore to play though, you just need to build stuff.

## What do I actually get?

Here's what you actually get:

- For every sidequest (or original project) you ship, you earn pixels. One hour of shipped work is **50 pixels**, plus the prize attached to that sidequest.
- Pixels can be spent in the shop on real prizes, from stickers all the way up to a MacBook Air.
- You also get starter guides, free hosting, tools, and yeah, stickers too.

If you're ever stuck, search it up first. Whatever you're wondering, someone's probably already asked it in `#pixl`. If that doesn't get you an answer, just ask.

---

## 1. Need inspiration?

Not sure what to build yet? That's completely normal.

Go look through the sidequests. Somewhere out there, an NPC already has a problem waiting for you to solve.

Or just build your own thing. That counts too.

### Software ideas

- [GitHub Trending](https://github.com/trending) to see what people are shipping right now.
- [DevPost](https://devpost.com/) for hackathon projects and app ideas.
- [Product Hunt](https://www.producthunt.com/) for tools you could put your own spin on.
- [Awesome Lists](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome) for collections of resources on basically any programming topic.

### Hardware ideas

- [Adafruit](https://www.adafruit.com/) has great electronics guides for basically every skill level.
- [Hackaday](https://hackaday.com/) has loads of projects, including some made by Hack Clubbers.
- [Instructables](https://www.instructables.com/) is full of things to get ideas from.
- [Printables](https://www.printables.com/) has 3D models you can use for inspiration. Don't just copy them though, make them yours.

---

## 2. What do you want to build?

Software, hardware, or some random idea nobody asked for — it doesn't matter.

Pick something and start.

You don't have to stick with one thing forever. Lots of people build different types of projects, so just start with whatever sounds interesting.

**Software** → websites, apps, games, bots. Get set up with VS Code, GitHub, and Hackatime.

**Hardware** → PCBs, 3D printing, electronics. Get set up with CAD and PCB tools.

---

## 3. Ship it!

Once you've actually built something, ship it.

Shipping means submitting your project for review. That's how you get pixels and your prize.

Check what a good submission looks like before you send yours in.

---

## 4. Free stuff to help you build

You shouldn't have to spend your own money on this.

Hack Club covers:

- Free AI credits for building your own agents and tools. Not for running some pre-built coding assistant.
- Free hosting on Nest, a Linux server you can put basically anything on.
- Free domains — open a PR on the DNS repo and claim one.
- Free CDN for images, video, and whatever assets you need.

---

## 5. Need more help?

Getting stuck is normal. You're not stuck alone.

- Ask in `#pixl` on Slack. People there are building the same kind of stuff you are.
- Search first. Seriously, whatever it is has probably already come up before.
- If you still can't find an answer, ask.

> **Tip:** The best rule is: search it up. Whatever you're stuck on, someone's probably already solved it.

> **Warning:** Don't just ship a copy of a tutorial. Make it yours. That's what reviewers actually look for, and it's what makes building fun.

